<python>
cs_content_header = cs_long_name
</python>

Images, PDF handouts, data files, stylesheets, scripts, and other files that
CAT-SOOP should deliver without processing belong in a directory named
`__STATIC__`.  Page source refers to them with logical URLs such as
`CURRENT/plot.png` or `COURSE/images/logo.svg`; CAT-SOOP rewrites those URLs to
the appropriate page or static-file address.

<tableofcontents/>

<catsoop-section>Organizing Static Files</catsoop-section>

A course can have static files at its root and alongside individual page
directories:

```text
numerical_methods/
├── preload.py
├── content.catsoop
├── __STATIC__/
│   ├── images/
│   │   └── course-logo.svg
│   └── handouts/
│       └── syllabus.pdf
└── homework/
    └── hw01/
        ├── content.catsoop
        └── __STATIC__/
            ├── convergence.png
            ├── starter.py
            └── data/
                └── measurements.csv
```

The root `__STATIC__` directory is useful for course-wide assets.  A page's
own `__STATIC__` directory keeps figures, downloads, and supporting files next
to the source that uses them.  Static subdirectories such as `images` and
`data` can be nested as deeply as needed.

Do not place an image directly beside `content.catsoop`:

```text
hw01/
├── content.catsoop
└── convergence.png        # wrong location
```

Move it into `hw01/__STATIC__/convergence.png`.  CAT-SOOP treats files outside
`__STATIC__` as course source or support files, not as public static content.

When resolving a course static URL, CAT-SOOP looks for the closest applicable
`__STATIC__` directory along that URL's page path.  This lets pages use nearby
assets while a course-level `__STATIC__` holds shared assets.  Be aware that a
nearer `__STATIC__` directory takes precedence; creating one can shadow the
ancestor location for that path.

<div class="bs-callout bs-callout-warning">
<b>Static files are public resources.</b>  CAT-SOOP handles requests under
<code>/_static/</code> before loading the course page or authenticating the
visitor.  Do not put solutions, private student data, credentials, or anything
else access-controlled in <code>__STATIC__</code>.
</div>

<catsoop-section>Images in Markdown</catsoop-section>

On the `homework/hw01` page in the tree above, Markdown can display the
page-local image with `CURRENT`:

```markdown
![Convergence of the iterative method](CURRENT/convergence.png)
```

The text in square brackets becomes the image's alternative text.  Write a
short description that conveys the figure's purpose rather than repeating a
caption word for word.

The same page can use a course-wide image with `COURSE`:

```markdown
![Numerical Methods course logo](COURSE/images/course-logo.svg)
```

Images can also be links.  This example displays a thumbnail and links it to
the full-size image:

```markdown
[![Convergence plot](CURRENT/convergence-small.png)](CURRENT/convergence.png)
```

Standard external URLs continue to work and are not changed:

```markdown
![Organization logo](https://example.edu/assets/logo.svg)
```

<catsoop-section>Images With HTML</catsoop-section>

Use an HTML `<img>` element when you need attributes that Markdown does not
conveniently express, such as a width, CSS class, or lazy loading:

```html
<img src="CURRENT/convergence.png"
     alt="Error falls rapidly for ten iterations and then levels off"
     width="720"
     loading="lazy">
```

A responsive image can be written as:

```html
<figure>
  <img src="COURSE/images/course-logo.svg"
       alt="Numerical Methods"
       style="max-width: 100%; height: auto;">
  <figcaption>The logo shared by every page in the course.</figcaption>
</figure>
```

CAT-SOOP rewrites the `src` attribute after it has converted Markdown and
processed the page's custom tags.  This is why the same special prefixes work
in both Markdown-generated images and literal HTML.

As described in [Page Rendering and Request Data](COURSE/docs/authoring/rendering),
avoid blank lines inside a raw HTML block.  A blank line can make the Markdown
parser end the HTML block earlier than intended.

<catsoop-section>Downloads and Other Assets</catsoop-section>

Use an ordinary Markdown link for a downloadable file:

```markdown
Download the [starter program](CURRENT/starter.py) and the
[measurement data](CURRENT/data/measurements.csv).

Read the [course syllabus](COURSE/handouts/syllabus.pdf).
```

The equivalent raw HTML is:

```html
<a href="CURRENT/data/measurements.csv" download>
  Download the measurements as CSV
</a>
```

The `download` attribute asks the browser to save the resource rather than
navigate to it.  Browser behavior can still depend on the file type and
browser settings.

Linked CSS files use the same rewriting mechanism:

```html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="COURSE/css/course-figures.css">
```

CAT-SOOP rewrites URLs in `href` attributes on links and stylesheets, and in
`src` attributes on images and scripts.  It does not rewrite arbitrary text
or a URL inside a CSS `style` attribute.  For example,
`style="background-image: url(CURRENT/background.png)"` does not receive this
special handling; prefer an `<img>` element, a linked stylesheet, or a URL
generated explicitly in page Python.

<catsoop-section>Special URL Prefixes</catsoop-section>

The principal prefixes available to course authors are:

<table class="table table-striped">
<thead><tr><th>Prefix</th><th>Starting point</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>CURRENT</code></td><td>The directory containing the current page</td><td>Page-local images, downloads, child pages, and nearby resources</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>COURSE</code></td><td>The current course's root directory</td><td>Shared course assets and links that should work from every page</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>BASE</code></td><td>The CAT-SOOP installation</td><td>Built-in CAT-SOOP assets and utility pages</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

For a directory page, `CURRENT` starts at the directory holding
`content.catsoop`.  For a single-file page such as `homework/overview.md`, it
starts at the containing `homework` directory.

For example, on `/numerical_methods/homework/hw01`:

```markdown
[A child page](CURRENT/extensions)
[Homework 2](CURRENT/../hw02)
[Course calendar](COURSE/calendar)
[CAT-SOOP license](BASE/_util/license)
```

CAT-SOOP first checks whether a `CURRENT` or `COURSE` target is a dynamic page.
If it is, the rewritten URL points to that page.  Otherwise the rewritten URL
points below `/_static/`, where CAT-SOOP looks in the relevant `__STATIC__`
directory.  Thus the author uses the same logical form for page links and
static resources:

```markdown
[Homework 2 page](COURSE/homework/hw02)
![Homework 2 diagram](COURSE/homework/hw02/diagram.png)
```

Query strings and fragments are retained during rewriting:

```markdown
[Filtered results](CURRENT/report?section=lab#results)
[Week 2 on the calendar](COURSE/calendar?week=2#monday)
```

Use `CURRENT` when the relationship should move with the page directory.  Use
`COURSE` when the target has a stable course-root location.  Use `BASE`
sparingly in course content because it couples the course to resources
provided by a particular CAT-SOOP installation.

<catsoop-subsection>Extension Prefixes</catsoop-subsection>

CAT-SOOP also recognizes `_qtype`, `_handler`, `_auth`, and `_plugin` as
static-resource prefixes.  They address files in an installed question type,
page handler, authentication module, or plugin.  For example, a question type
implementation might load:

```html
<img src="_qtype/custom_graph/icon.svg"
     alt="Custom graph question">
```

These prefixes are mainly for extension developers.  Ordinary course content
should normally use `CURRENT` or `COURSE`; a course should not depend on an
extension's private assets unless that extension documents them as a supported
interface.

<catsoop-section>What the Browser Receives</catsoop-section>

Assume `cs_url_root` is `https://catsoop.example` and the current page is
`/numerical_methods/homework/hw01`.  Conceptually, CAT-SOOP rewrites:

```text
CURRENT/convergence.png
    → https://catsoop.example/_static/numerical_methods/homework/hw01/convergence.png

COURSE/images/course-logo.svg
    → https://catsoop.example/_static/numerical_methods/images/course-logo.svg

COURSE/homework/hw02
    → https://catsoop.example/numerical_methods/homework/hw02

BASE/js/ace/ace.js
    → https://catsoop.example/_static/_base/js/ace/ace.js
```

The exact leading URL can differ when CAT-SOOP is mounted below another path
or configured as a single-course site.  That is the reason to keep
`CURRENT`, `COURSE`, and `BASE` in source instead of hard-coding deployment
URLs.

<catsoop-section>Troubleshooting</catsoop-section>

If an image or download returns 404:

1. confirm that the file is inside the intended `__STATIC__` directory;
2. check capitalization—production servers commonly use case-sensitive
   filenames;
3. interpret `CURRENT` from the page's directory, not from the course root;
4. try `COURSE/path/from/course/root` for a shared file;
5. inspect the rendered element in the browser to see the rewritten URL; and
6. check whether a nearer `__STATIC__` directory is shadowing an ancestor.

Keep filenames URL-friendly.  Lowercase names with hyphens or underscores
avoid quoting and case problems across browsers and operating systems.
